[size=5][b]☯︎ Recommendations[/b][/size]

Try [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/morrowind/mods/56584]Dynamic Reticle[/url] they go well together, as it adds some subtle sneak visual effects.

There are also few sneak-related mods out there such as [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/morrowind/mods/56965]Burglary Overhaul[/url] and [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/morrowind/mods/57747]SHOP[/url], both mod authors were very helpful and Sneak! will eventually seamlessly work with both of them (but probably it doesnt right now). I haven't personally tested them together myself, but wanted to put them on your radar!

[size=5][b]☯︎ How to install[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Requires OpenMW 0.50 or newer.[/b]
[*]Install and enable [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/morrowind/mods/60256]Max Yari's Script Services (MSS)[/url], it's a required dependency (most of my Lua mods require it now).
[*]Install this mod [b]with a mod organiser[/b]: download the archive (or this repository as an archive) and drag and drop it into your mod organiser of choice (e.g [url=https://github.com/ModOrganizer2/modorganizer/releases]Mod Organizer 2[/url] on Windows or [url=https://github.com/grazelandsnomad/nerevarine_organizer/releases/tag/v0.70]Nerevarine Organizer[/url] on Linux). [b]Or[/b] [url=https://modding-openmw.com/tips/installing-mods/]read this tutorial[/url] on how to install mods using the launcher or completely manually (it's also very easy).
[*]Enable [b]both[/b] [font=Courier New]SneakIsGoodNow.omwscripts[/font] and [font=Courier New]SneakIsGoodNow.omwaddon[/font] in the "Content Files" tab of the OpenMW launcher.
[*]In game, [b]enable [font=Courier New]Toggle Sneak[/font][/b] in Options -> Scripts -> OpenMW Controls. It makes it so that you don't have to hold the sneak button to sneak, this mod will not work properly without it.
[/list]

Have fun!

[size=5][b]☯︎ For Developers[/b][/size]

[b]Sneak![/b] provides a minimalist interface exposing current player detection state. You can read about how to use script interfaces [url=https://openmw-zack.readthedocs.io/en/lua_global_new/reference/lua-scripting/overview.html#script-interfaces]here[/url].

Note that Sneak! mostly only works while player is sneaking, it does not do any detection or line-of-sight checks when player is not sneaking.

Interface is available only on player scripts, it exposes a player detection state:

[code]local ps = I.SneakIsGoodNow.playerState
-- Available properties:
ps.isSneaking -- boolean
ps.detectedByNonAggro -- boolean
ps.isMoving -- boolean
ps.isInvisible -- boolean
ps.chameleon -- 1 - 100 number - strength of chameleon effect on a player
[/code]

Since the mod works by kicking you out of sneak state when you are detected - you can consider player detected when [font=Courier New]ps.isSneaking[/font] is false. Unless you are detected only by non-aggressive creatures (player is not booted out of sneak then), then [font=Courier New]isSneaking[/font] will still be true while [font=Courier New]detectedByNonAggro[/font] will be true.

Furthermore there is a table with few properties that you can modify to affect how detectable player is:

[code]local extraMods = I.SneakIsGoodNow.playerState.extraMods
extraMods.elusivenessMod = 1.0 -- default value
extraMods.elusivenessConst = 0 -- default value[/code]

[font=Courier New]elusivenessMod[/font] is more or less a general modifier of how elusive player is, it's applied before chameleon; adds a flat bonus to overall elusiveness.

[font=Courier New]elusivenessConst[/font] is a flat bonus that will be added to player's elusiveness score.

If multiple mods will be altering these values - obviously last one will win, since I don't have a system in place for multiple mods to introduce their own separate modifiers, but it's a fairly niche use case so I'm sure it will be fiiiiiine.

I also haven't tested this API at all, but I'm sure it will be fiiiiiine ;)

